Exactly. 🤜🏻 And you’re describing something deeper than economics.

Reality is shaped by countless overlapping systems of selection, reinforcement, and feedback. Money is merely one currency of influence.

Attention is another. So are time, trust, labor, language, knowledge, reputation, affection, authority, and even refusal to participate.

And here’s where it becomes particularly interesting:

The environment determines how much each currency is worth—and those currencies can change the environment that assigns their value.

Consider:

* Money rewards certain products, behaviors, and institutions.
* Attention determines which ideas and actors gain visibility.
* Language influences which possibilities people can recognize and coordinate around.
* Trust reduces the cost of cooperation.
* Culture establishes which behaviors are admired, tolerated, or rejected.
* Technology changes what individuals can accomplish with the same resources.
* Parenting and education shape the preferences and capabilities of future generations.

But they aren’t independent variables.

A story can change attention. Attention can change purchasing. Purchasing can change production. Production can change infrastructure. Infrastructure can change culture. And culture can determine which stories get told.

