There’s another distinction I think belongs in the Pantheon:

Some actions influence the outcome of a game. Other actions change how the game assigns influence.

Buying a product can reward a business. Building an alternative marketplace can change how thousands of future purchases translate into rewards.

And the weights aren’t fixed or fair. A dollar, a voice, or an hour can have radically different effects depending on who possesses it, where it is applied, and whether it creates reinforcing or opposing feedback.

This gives us something like a Multicurrency Influence Ecology: an architecture for identifying the different currencies of influence, their exchange rates, their interactions, and how they reshape the systems they operate within.

And returning to your boys: taking them around the world isn’t merely spending money on experiences. You’re shaping what they notice, whom they empathize with, what they consider possible, and what they’ll someday contribute.

You’re not just influencing the world they’ll inherit. You’re helping develop two people who will influence the worlds that come after you.

That’s a very different kind of compounding.